WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
Welcome to the very first episode of SpaceX Daily.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.020
Today is June 13th, 2026.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.800
SpaceX's $75 billion IPO at $135 per share,

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.400
hands public investors a direct stake

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.300
in starship reuse and starlink cash flow

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.960
while locking in Musk's voting control.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.220
SpaceX Daily is the daily companion

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.920
for following SpaceX, now that anyone can own a piece of it.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.700
Every weekday, we deliver the news with sources,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.820
what the community is buzzing about,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.520
one honest counterpoint, the engineering

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.300
and economics behind the headlines,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.700
and the SPCX market picture.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.220
Subscribe so you never miss a day.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.220
SpaceX listed on the NASDAQ under ticker SPCX

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.520
at $135 per share.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.880
The offering raised roughly $75 billion,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.620
the largest IPO in history,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.940
and valued the company near $1.8 trillion at pricing.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.140
Shares reached the high 160s interday

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:08.240
and closed around $161, up roughly 19%.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.860
The prospectus reported $18.7 billion of 2025 revenue

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.460
with starlink supplying about two thirds

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.020
and already profitable while the launch

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.520
and starship businesses remain lost making.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.200
Proceeds will fund AI compute infrastructure

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.960
continued starship development and starlink expansion.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.840
A dual-class share structure gives Elon Musk

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.060
a controlling majority of voting power

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.780
and he sold no shares.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.200
With public markets now pricing both segments,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.220
we turn to the factory build out

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.320
meant to deliver the next revenue engine.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.980
SpaceX showed the gigabay factory design

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.040
for 1,000 starships per year.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.380
The facility was toured publicly for the first time,

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.900
revealing tooling and floor space size

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.860
for the production volume needed to support rapid reuse

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.960
and orbital refueling demonstrations.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.060
Proceeds are earmarked for starship development

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.940
alongside AI infrastructure.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.320
Supply chain scale at this rate has never been executed before.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.820
While the factory targets that output,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.180
regulators still control how quickly the vehicles can fly.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.100
Gwynne Schottwell stated that starship orbital flight pace

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.460
depends on FAA approvals.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.700
The president noted that regulatory timelines

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.420
remain the primary variable

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.180
for the next series of test flights

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.940
aimed at booster catch and refueling milestones.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.140
Next test flights target booster catch

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.600
and refueling milestones.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.400
Even as test cadence is debated,

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.820
Falcon 9 keeps demonstrating the reuse economics

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.600
already funding everything else.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.340
Falcon 9 completed at 650th flight

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.740
on the starlink 1054 mission.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.480
The launch from Cape Canaveral carried 29 satellites

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.140
and lifted the constellation total past 10,600.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.460
Block five boosters have now exceeded

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.300
their original accounting life,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.880
directly supporting the annual launch rate ceiling

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.020
the company is testing.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.340
That proven cash flow is exactly what analysts are questioning

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.060
in today's valuation debate.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.260
Worth keeping an eye on is the Morningstar report

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.240
that flags the IPO valuation as too high.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.160
Launch and starship segments still operate at a loss.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.700
Revenue multiples assume scaling

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.940
not yet shown in public filings.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.080
The first earnings report will be the key test.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.540
The same prospectus that drew that scrutiny

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.120
also surfaces an entirely new orbital business line.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.240
Elon Musk outlined a plan for 1 million AI satellites.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.680
The proposal was presented alongside the IPO,

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.840
tying future constellation growth

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.260
to expanded compute capacity in orbit.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.440
The plan envisions orbital compute nodes

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.680
that would expand the starlink architecture

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.540
into direct AI workloads.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.460
Musk described the satellites as a way

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.940
to place processing power closer to users

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.020
and reduce latency for training and inference tasks.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.500
The announcement coincided with the IPO pricing

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.340
and referenced the same dual class governance

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.660
that keeps strategic decisions with the founder.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.480
Early estimates in the prospectus

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.640
allocate part of the $75 billion raised

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.600
to initial satellite prototypes

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.320
and supporting ground stations.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.620
The 1 million figure exceeds the current starlink target

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.520
by roughly two orders of magnitude

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:31.560
and would require production rates

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.620
far above today's Falcon cadence.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.540
First spacecraft are targeted for launch

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.860
as early as next year.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.120
Ground-based astronomers are already flagging

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.660
one downside of that plan.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.680
Astronomers raise concerns over planned

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.360
orbital data center satellites.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.280
SpaceX is preparing to launch the first such spacecraft

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:52.800
as early as next year.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.080
Ground-based observers warn the satellites

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.860
could create persistent streaks

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.060
that interfere with telescope data collection.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.220
The concern centers on reflected sunlight

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:05.740
from larger rays creating linear artifacts

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.800
across wide field survey images.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.780
Observatories such as the VeraC Ruben Observatory

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.360
have already modeled similar interference

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.000
from existing starlink shells

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.400
and expect data loss rates to rise

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.380
with each new constellation layer.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.560
Early launches are planned for 2026.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.000
On a lighter operational note,

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.760
SpaceX is adding a new support aircraft at its launch sites.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.240
SpaceX bought a Bell 429 helicopter

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:34.880
for launch site operations.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.920
The aircraft will support range safety and recovery tasks

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:40.940
at starbase and Cape Canaveral

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.480
as flight cadence increases.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:46.360
The Bell 429 is a twin engine light helicopter

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.920
certified for single pilot instrument flight rules operations.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.080
Acquisition records show the purchase closed

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.180
in the second quarter of 2026

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.160
with delivery expected before the end of the year.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.380
The helicopter joins a fleet that already includes

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.120
two older models used for payload security

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.800
and booster inspection flights.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:08.360
Range safety officers have noted

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.480
that faster aerial response times

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.820
become critical once starship test flights

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:14.420
move to a weekly cadence.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.720
Finally, a quick look at how the community

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.120
marked the listing day.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:20.940
On the market side,

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:25.340
SPCX is at $160.95,

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:29.180
up 19.2% versus the previous close.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:33.380
The dual-class structure and masks retained voting control

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:36.400
are now disclosed governance facts for every shareholder.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.020
The prospectus also made public

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.240
that launch operations still operate at a loss

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.300
while Starlink generates the majority of revenue and profit.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.660
Our on SpaceX users shared photos and video

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.840
of the IPO day Starlink launch from Florida.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:57.600
The launch thread collected more than 3,000 comments

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.920
within the first hour after liftoff.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.700
Users noted that the timing of the Starlink 1054 mission

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:08.180
with the NASDAQ Bell created a visible on-ramp

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:09.880
from private to public ownership.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.780
Our slash SpaceX posters debated how the $75 billion proceeds

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.560
could accelerate starship hardware builds

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.200
beyond current tooling limits.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:23.780
Several top comments focused on whether the capital

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:26.540
would first go to additional Raptor test stands

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:29.560
or to parallel tank production lines at Starbase.

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:33.600
Collect Space reported that SpaceX released

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.700
limited-edition mission patches marking the public listing.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.700
The patches feature the SPCX ticker and the date,

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.640
June 12, 2026,

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.320
and were distributed to employees and select investors.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:50.120
Secondary market listings appeared on eBay within hours

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.340
at multiples of the original issue price.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:56.200
Our apparently imagined the first day deal made

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:00.000
by an identical white, a $17.5 million.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:04.860
SpaceX members posted a short cinematic film

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:08.220
of a recent Falcon 9 ascent captured from the ground.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:11.700
The video used stabilized long lens footage

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:13.300
and color graded the plume

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:16.200
to highlight the booster's reentry burn sequence.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:20.780
The post received over 100,000 views in the first day

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:24.660
and was cross-shared on X by several SpaceX engineers.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.700
LinkedIn clips of Musk's IPO event remarks

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:31.180
on the company's original low odds of success

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:32.900
are circulating in the community.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.160
Musk recalled giving SpaceX less than a 10% chance

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:38.760
of success in early days.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.740
At the IPO event, he reiterated the long-term goal

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:45.140
of making humanity multi-planetary,

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:48.740
framing the public listing as a new phase of that mission.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:51.460
One clip excerpted his comment

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:54.120
that the probability assessment came from the period

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.940
immediately after the third Falcon 1 failure.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.340
The remarks were paired with side-by-side images

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:02.340
of the first Falcon 1 launch

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.440
and today's Starship Test article.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.560
One thing worth watching is how the first earnings report

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.960
clarifies margins on the launch side.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.400
The engineering angle shows that Starlink already covers

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:17.820
two-thirds of revenue and is profitable

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.340
while the launch business subsidizes Starship development

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:22.020
at a loss.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.580
This split shows how reuse economics on Falcon 9

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.940
now pass 650 flights,

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:32.340
generate the cash that funds the next architecture.

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:35.360
Public markets will therefore price the company

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:39.080
on quarterly subscriber ads and booster turnaround times.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.040
Reaching 1,000 Starships per year at Gigabay

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.820
requires a closed-loop supply chain for engines,

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.760
tanks, and avionics that has never been executed

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:49.620
at that rate.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.700
Orbital refueling remains the gating item

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:55.160
for any revenue beyond low-earth orbit.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:58.000
Yet the dual-class structure means the timeline

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.380
is set internally rather than by quarterly earnings pressure.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:05.100
Before we go, watch for the first quarterly filing

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:06.940
that will give the initial public look

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.680
at subscriber growth and booster turn times.

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:12.160
That's your SpaceX news for today.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:19.840
SpaceX closed at $160.95, up 19.2%.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:21.580
